WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:04.502
A sci-fi legend is back in "Avatar:&nbsp;The Way of Water" — somehow! 

00:00:04.502 --> 00:00:07.020
So&nbsp;how does that work? Spoilers ahead!

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.800
"Avatar" fans will of course recall&nbsp;
that Sigourney Weaver's character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.180
the groundbreaking 2009 film, Dr. Grace&nbsp;
Augustine, died near the end of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.840
after being fatally wounded during an&nbsp;
escape. So when it was announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.800
the "Aliens" star would be reuniting with&nbsp;
James Cameron and company for the sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.060
shortly after the first film's release, it&nbsp;
obviously left fans scratching their heads.

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:28.986
"What's wrong with this picture?!"

00:00:28.986 --> 00:00:33.660
Sequel "Avatar: The Way of Water" not&nbsp;
only revisits Jake Sully and Neytiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.440
but also introduces us to their&nbsp;
Na'vi children including Lo'ak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.420
Neteyam and Tuk. Possibly more significant than&nbsp;
any of their biological offspring, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.020
is their adopted Na'vi daughter&nbsp;
Kiri, played by Sigourney Weaver.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.360
The biological daughter of Dr. Augustine, Kiri&nbsp;
is taken in by Sully and his family and comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.280
along with them as they must leave their forest&nbsp;
home and live amongst the Metkayina ocean clan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.260
Kiri is more than an excuse to bring Weaver back&nbsp;
into the franchise, however, as the character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.760
significance to "Way of Water" could very&nbsp;
well signify big things to come down the line.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.100
Kiri is introduced early on in "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.180
The Way of Water" as Dr. Augustine's daughter&nbsp;
— although rather than being born human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.553
she is a full-on Na'vi from the start. As director&nbsp;
James Cameron explained to Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:23.892 --> 00:01:27.840
This also plays into Kiri's character,&nbsp;
as she longs to connect with her deceased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.020
mother while remaining self-conscious&nbsp;
about living up to her Na'vi family.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.700
She has a special connection to the environment&nbsp;
around her, which takes on a new life when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.040
and Sully's family are forced out of their home&nbsp;
by a vengeful Colonel Quaritch and make base with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.840
the Metkayina people. As Kiri explores the lush&nbsp;
oceans and comes face-to-face with the water's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.240
colorful inhabitants, she also finds new ways to&nbsp;
connect and utilizes her abilities in unique ways.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.620
She not only connects with the underwater Tree&nbsp;
of Souls and claims to hear the heartbeat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.400
the deity Eywa, but is also able to&nbsp;
communicate with the sea creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.440
which comes in handy during the film's climax.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.880
Kiri's abilities make her stand out enough&nbsp;
from her motion-captured co-stars, but it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.620
her viewpoint on life that could spell a bigger&nbsp;
picture for the upcoming "Avatar" sequels. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.060
end of "The Way of Water," we know that the war&nbsp;
between the Na'vi and humanity is far from over.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.160
But just as Colonel Quaritch's son Spider, who&nbsp;
shares a close bond with Kiri, has love for both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.660
the human and Na'vi world, Kiri's non-violent&nbsp;
ways and connection with the world around her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.460
could turn the tide by showcasing a different&nbsp;
way around the seemingly never-ending conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.140
As the "Avatar" franchise is very much about&nbsp;
humanity's exploitation of natural resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.400
Kiri could be the key to unlocking how to&nbsp;
better utilize those elements for good.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.680
On top of that, we still don't know who Kiri's&nbsp;
father is, or even if she has one. We know she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.000
has her limits, as is evident when she suffers a&nbsp;
seizure while trying to connect to the underwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.680
Tree of Souls, so learning more about her&nbsp;
origins might be able to help figure out why.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.820
Kiri is one of the many mysteries in "The&nbsp;
Way of Water," but she's one that Sigourney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.140
Weaver is prepared to explore. As she&nbsp;
says in a recent interview with D23,

